window.PORTAL={"day": 15, "title": "Genetics, Dysmorphology & Inborn Errors of Metabolism", "docTitle": "", "subtitle": ["NEET-SS + ICP Paediatrics — Day 15", "Genetics, Dysmorphology and Inborn Errors of Metabolism — Consolidated Study Edition"], "source": "source/Day15_NEETSS_ICP_Genetics_IEM_Consolidated_Final.docx", "pages": [{"id": "overview", "title": "Overview", "group": "Start", "kind": "overview", "sections": [{"title": "", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "<strong>NEET-SS + ICP Paediatrics — Day 15</strong>"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Genetics, Dysmorphology and Inborn Errors of Metabolism — Consolidated Study Edition"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Tuesday 13 October 2026 • 75 base MCQs + 2 carry-over retests + 8 gap-fill extras = 85 questions • 6 data stations • 7 calculation drills"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Consolidated from the earlier Day 15 study edition and the supplied V4. Clinical protocols and high-risk thresholds require named-source verification; an editorial merge is not specialist certification."}, {"t": "callout", "label": "", "title": "", "html": ["<strong>What changed from V3</strong>", "<strong>Option-length bias reduced:</strong> 23 stems were rewritten with longer, plausible near-miss distractors. The correct option is now the unique longest in 17 of 85 items (20%), against 49% in V3. Letters: A 22, B 22, C 20, D 21; no more than 2 identical in a row.", "<strong>Stations are now testable:</strong> the six table-rows with the answer beside the data are replaced by data stations (blood-gas/ammonia panel, acylcarnitine bar profiles, copy-number plot, imprinting/SNP table, urea-cycle biomarker chart, newborn-screen decisions). Answers are in the key.", "<strong>Gap-fill (A11–A16 and 8 extra MCQs):</strong> Fragile X and repeat expansions, Duchenne, trisomy 13/18, Robertsonian recurrence, hyperammonaemia drugs, homocystinuria, CAH, mitochondrial syndromes, newborn-screening context and counselling.", "<strong>Calculation drill:</strong> three genetics/metabolic drills added (anion gap, ammonia unit conversion, scavenger loading dose); the Day 10 GIR repeats are kept as the original items.", "<strong>Carry-overs relabelled:</strong> the two retests (Q19, Q56) and the Day 11 recall block are labelled and renumbered C1–C13 so IDs no longer collide with the genetics list.", "<strong>Framework named:</strong> Q39 now says it follows the CHOP pathway. The CHARGE stem (Q31) no longer lists posterior embryotoxon, which fits Alagille/Axenfeld–Rieger better."], "fill": "E8F1F6"}, {"t": "p", "html": "EDITORIAL MERGE NOTE — V4 retained as the core for broader topic coverage and graphics. Earlier edition used for additional explanation checks. Q8 and Q12 answer keys corrected. See verification notes at end."}, {"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["Select the genomic assay from the suspected variant class and phenotype.", "Distinguish Down, Turner, Klinefelter, 22q11.2, Williams, Prader-Willi and Angelman syndromes, and the repeat-expansion and X-linked disorders.", "Use major/minor anomalies and developmental mechanisms (sequence, association) in dysmorphology.", "Interpret acid-base state, glucose, ketones, lactate, ammonia and acylcarnitines in a sick neonate; stabilise before the molecular diagnosis.", "Map OTC, CPS1/NAGS, ASS1, ASL and ARG1 defects by citrulline, orotic acid and argininosuccinate.", "Recognise treatable aminoacidopathies, organic acidaemias, FAODs, lysosomal, peroxisomal and mitochondrial disorders.", "Interpret newborn-screen positives without treating screening as diagnosis."]}, {"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Objective</strong>", "<strong>Cases / stations</strong>", "<strong>Question focus</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>Genomic testing and inheritance</strong>", "Cases 1–2, Stations 3, 4", "Base Q1–Q14 and extras"], ["<strong>Chromosomal syndromes</strong>", "Cases 2–3", "Base Q15–Q33"], ["<strong>IEM triage and hyperammonaemia</strong>", "Cases 4–5, Stations 1, 5", "Base Q34–Q48"], ["<strong>Biomarkers and therapies</strong>", "Cases 6–7, Stations 2, 6", "Base Q49–Q69"], ["<strong>Newborn screening</strong>", "Cases 7–8, Station 6", "Base Q70–Q77"]]}]}]}, {"id": "part-1", "title": "Notes", "eyebrow": "Part A", "group": "Study Notes", "kind": "notes", "sections": [{"title": "", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Tag ⚑ verify = a dose, threshold or attribution I could not confirm against source in this session."}]}, {"title": "A1. Genetic diagnostic strategy", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Start with phenotype: prenatal/perinatal history, three-generation pedigree, consanguinity, recurrent loss, growth, head circumference, development, major and minor anomalies and a targeted examination. Decide the likely variant class: aneuploidy, copy-number change, balanced rearrangement, imprinting/UPD, repeat expansion or single-gene variant."}, {"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Assay</strong>", "<strong>Best for</strong>", "<strong>Blind spot</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>Karyotype</strong>", "Aneuploidy, large structural change, balanced translocation, mosaicism", "Small deletions, sequence variants, repeat expansions"], ["<strong>Chromosomal microarray / SNP array</strong>", "Genome-wide CNVs; regions of homozygosity (isodisomy)", "Balanced rearrangements, most point variants, repeat expansions"], ["<strong>Exome / genome sequencing (trio)</strong>", "Monogenic variants; de novo and recessive disease", "Structural/non-coding causes; coverage limits"], ["<strong>Methylation / MS-MLPA</strong>", "Imprinting at 15q11.2-q13", "Not a broad test; misses UBE3A sequence variants"], ["<strong>Targeted repeat analysis</strong>", "Fragile X (CGG), Huntington, myotonic dystrophy", "Specific to the locus tested"], ["<strong>MLPA / sequencing of one gene</strong>", "Deletion/duplication or sequence in a candidate gene (e.g. DMD)", "Needs a strong candidate"]]}, {"t": "p", "html": "ACMG 2021 supports exome/genome sequencing as a first- or second-tier test for congenital anomalies, developmental delay or intellectual disability; a fixed “CMA always first” rule is outdated, and the choice depends on question, access and local practice."}]}, {"title": "A2. Inheritance and imprinting", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["Autosomal recessive: carrier × carrier → 25% affected, 50% carrier per pregnancy.", "X-linked recessive: carrier mother → 50% of sons affected; 25% of all children are affected sons with an equal sex ratio.", "Autosomal dominant: an affected heterozygote usually transmits at 50%; variable penetrance and expression matter.", "Mitochondrial: maternal transmission; heteroplasmy and the bottleneck explain variable severity.", "Imprinting: Prader-Willi = loss of paternally expressed 15q11.2-q13 genes; Angelman = loss of maternal neuronal UBE3A. Methylation shows the pattern; SNP array separates deletion from isodisomy; UBE3A sequence variants can have normal methylation.", "UPD isodisomy can unmask recessive variants despite normal chromosome dosage."]}]}, {"title": "A3. Dysmorphology language", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Term</strong>", "<strong>Meaning</strong>", "<strong>Example</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>Syndrome</strong>", "Pattern with a shared known cause", "Trisomy 21"], ["<strong>Sequence</strong>", "Cascade from one primary event", "Oligohydramnios → pulmonary hypoplasia, contractures"], ["<strong>Association</strong>", "Non-random cluster without one cause", "VACTERL"], ["<strong>Major anomaly</strong>", "Functionally significant structural defect", "AV septal defect"], ["<strong>Minor anomaly</strong>", "Little standalone impact; pattern informative", "Single palmar crease"]]}, {"t": "p", "html": "A dysmorphology work-up screens heart, renal tract, eyes, hearing, airway, growth and development, then selects the molecular test."}]}, {"title": "A4. Syndrome discriminators", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Syndrome</strong>", "<strong>Discriminating cluster</strong>", "<strong>Test / cue</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>Down (trisomy 21)</strong>", "Hypotonia, AVSD, facies, TAM (GATA1), OSA", "Karyotype for mechanism; echo, thyroid, hearing"], ["<strong>Turner (45,X / mosaic)</strong>", "Short stature, lymphoedema, coarctation, gonadal dysgenesis", "Karyotype; Y material → gonadoblastoma risk"], ["<strong>Klinefelter (47,XXY)</strong>", "Tall, small firm testes, raised FSH/LH", "Karyotype"], ["<strong>22q11.2 deletion</strong>", "Conotruncal defect, palate, hypocalcaemia, variable T-cell deficiency", "CMA; calcium; T-cell subsets before live vaccines"], ["<strong>Williams (7q11.23)</strong>", "SVAS, hypercalcaemia, sociability, visuospatial weakness", "CMA; arterial/anaesthesia risk"], ["<strong>Prader-Willi</strong>", "Neonatal hypotonia, later hyperphagia", "Methylation + SNP array"], ["<strong>Angelman</strong>", "Severe speech impairment, seizures, ataxia, frequent laughter", "Methylation, then UBE3A"], ["<strong>Cri-du-chat (5p−)</strong>", "Cat-like cry, microcephaly", "CMA/karyotype"], ["<strong>Wolf-Hirschhorn (4p−)</strong>", "Growth failure, seizures, “Greek helmet” face", "CMA"], ["<strong>Beckwith-Wiedemann</strong>", "Macroglossia, overgrowth, omphalocele, hypoglycaemia", "11p15 imprinting; tumour surveillance"], ["<strong>Noonan / RASopathy</strong>", "Pulmonary stenosis, short stature, hypertelorism", "RAS/MAPK panel or exome"]]}]}, {"title": "A5. The acutely ill neonate with possible IEM", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Classic picture: a well infant deteriorates after feeds with vomiting, poor feeding, lethargy, seizures, apnoea or encephalopathy. Treat sepsis concurrently; do not wait for it to be excluded."}, {"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>First-hour pattern</strong>", "<strong>Likely group</strong>", "<strong>First tests</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>Respiratory alkalosis + high ammonia, little ketosis</strong>", "Urea-cycle disorder", "Ammonia, plasma amino acids, urine orotic acid"], ["<strong>High-anion-gap acidosis + ketosis ± ammonia, neutropenia</strong>", "Organic acidaemia", "Urine organic acids, acylcarnitines, lactate"], ["<strong>Hypoketotic hypoglycaemia after fasting or illness</strong>", "Fatty-acid oxidation disorder", "Acylcarnitines, urine organic acids, critical samples"], ["<strong>Persistent lactic acidosis, multisystem disease</strong>", "Mitochondrial / pyruvate disorder", "Repeat lactate, pyruvate, organ tests"], ["<strong>Coarse facies, organomegaly, regression</strong>", "Lysosomal storage", "Enzyme assays, urine GAG"], ["<strong>Hypotonia, seizures, liver disease, raised VLCFA</strong>", "Peroxisomal biogenesis disorder", "VLCFA, peroxisomal markers"]]}, {"t": "callout", "label": "", "title": "", "html": ["<strong>First 60 minutes (Algorithm 1)</strong>", "Airway, breathing, circulation; treat seizures, shock, hypoglycaemia and suspected sepsis.", "Send glucose, gas, anion gap, lactate, ammonia (correct handling), liver tests, ketones; save extra samples.", "Start dextrose-containing anti-catabolic IV fluids (individual GIR); do not wait for rare tests.", "Stop protein in a suspected intoxication disorder only under metabolic advice and restart early; escalate severe or rising ammonia."], "fill": "E8F1F6"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Severe hyperammonaemia (Algorithm 2):</strong> confirm urgently without delaying treatment; high-calorie IV dextrose ± lipids; metabolic consultation; IV nitrogen scavengers and disorder-specific arginine/citrulline/carglumic acid; plan extracorporeal clearance at severe levels. <strong>CHOP categories (US institutional pathway, not universal thresholds):</strong> &lt;200 µmol/L mild without encephalopathy; 200–500 moderate; ≥500 or severe encephalopathy severe; suggested glucose infusion 10–15 mg/kg/min in selected categories."}]}, {"title": "A6. Urea-cycle disorders", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Defect</strong>", "<strong>Citrulline / orotic acid</strong>", "<strong>Clue</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>NAGS / CPS1</strong>", "Very low citrulline; low or normal orotic acid", "Carglumic acid for NAGS"], ["<strong>OTC</strong>", "Very low citrulline; HIGH urinary orotic acid", "X-linked"], ["<strong>ASS1 (citrullinaemia I)</strong>", "Very HIGH citrulline", "Block after citrulline"], ["<strong>ASL</strong>", "High argininosuccinic acid", "Hair and liver features"], ["<strong>ARG1</strong>", "High arginine", "Later spasticity"]]}]}, {"title": "A7. Organic acidaemias, aminoacidopathies and FAOD", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Disorder</strong>", "<strong>Biochemical clue</strong>", "<strong>Principle</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>Propionic / methylmalonic acidaemia</strong>", "C3 raised; ketoacidosis ± ammonia", "Stop catabolism; metabolic specialist; B12 trial in MMA"], ["<strong>Combined MMA + homocystinuria</strong>", "MMA and homocysteine raised; methionine low", "Intracellular cobalamin-processing defect"], ["<strong>MSUD</strong>", "Leucine, isoleucine, alloisoleucine; ketotic encephalopathy", "Rapidly lower leucine"], ["<strong>Classic galactosaemia</strong>", "Milk-triggered liver failure, E. coli sepsis, cataracts", "Remove galactose/lactose at once"], ["<strong>Hereditary fructose intolerance</strong>", "Fructose/sucrose → vomiting, hypoglycaemia, liver injury", "Avoid fructose, sucrose, sorbitol"], ["<strong>PKU</strong>", "High phenylalanine; check BH4 pathway", "Phenylalanine-controlled diet"], ["<strong>Tyrosinaemia I</strong>", "Succinylacetone; liver and renal Fanconi", "Nitisinone + diet"], ["<strong>MCAD</strong>", "C8 dominant; fasting hypoketotic hypoglycaemia", "Avoid fasting; emergency glucose"], ["<strong>VLCAD</strong>", "C14:1; cardiomyopathy, myopathy", "Individual fat/carbohydrate plan; anti-catabolism"]]}]}, {"title": "A8. Lysosomal, peroxisomal, mitochondrial patterns", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Disorder</strong>", "<strong>Discriminator</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>Pompe (GSD II)</strong>", "Infantile hypotonia, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, macroglossia"], ["<strong>GSD I</strong>", "Fasting hypoglycaemia, lactic acidosis, hyperuricaemia, hypertriglyceridaemia"], ["<strong>Gaucher type 1</strong>", "Hepatosplenomegaly, cytopenias, bone pain; no primary neuronopathy"], ["<strong>Niemann-Pick C</strong>", "Vertical supranuclear gaze palsy, ataxia, splenomegaly"], ["<strong>MPS I / II</strong>", "Iduronidase / iduronate sulfatase; corneal clouding prominent in I, usually absent in II (X-linked)"], ["<strong>Zellweger spectrum</strong>", "Neonatal hypotonia, seizures, liver disease, raised VLCFA"], ["<strong>X-linked ALD</strong>", "Boy with adrenal insufficiency, cerebral white-matter disease, raised VLCFA"], ["<strong>Biotinidase deficiency</strong>", "Seizures, dermatitis, alopecia; treatable with biotin"], ["<strong>Mitochondrial disease</strong>", "Maternal inheritance possible; heteroplasmy; variable lactate; multisystem"]]}]}, {"title": "A9. Newborn screening", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Dried-blood-spot tandem MS screens selected aminoacidopathies, organic acidaemias and FAODs; panels vary by programme. A positive screen needs prompt confirmatory testing; a normal screen does not exclude a symptomatic IEM. ACMG ACT sheets are US guidance, not an Indian universal panel. <strong>ISPAE 2018 (India) congenital hypothyroidism:</strong> ideally screen at 48–72 hours; TSH &gt;40 mIU/L (serum-equivalent) needs urgent venous FT4/T4 and TSH; treat without waiting for imaging; levothyroxine 10–15 µg/kg/day. Thresholds are assay-specific and should not be transferred between DBS laboratories without conversion."}]}, {"title": "A10. Numbers and formulas", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Fact</strong>", "<strong>Value / caveat</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>Autosomal recessive recurrence</strong>", "25% affected per pregnancy"], ["<strong>X-linked recessive carrier mother</strong>", "50% of sons; 25% of all offspring if equal sex ratio"], ["<strong>GIR (mg/kg/min)</strong>", "dextrose % × fluid (mL/kg/day) ÷ 144"], ["<strong>Anion gap</strong>", "Na − (Cl + HCO₃)"], ["<strong>Ammonia conversion</strong>", "µmol/L ≈ µg/dL × 0.587 ⚑ verify the laboratory’s factor"], ["<strong>CHOP severe hyperammonaemia</strong>", "≥500 µmol/L or severe encephalopathy (US institutional)"], ["<strong>ISPAE CH</strong>", "Screen 48–72 h; TSH &gt;40 serum-equivalent → urgent confirmation; LT4 10–15 µg/kg/day"], ["<strong>CMA vs karyotype</strong>", "CMA = copy number; karyotype = aneuploidy and balanced structure"]]}]}, {"title": "A11. Repeat-expansion and X-linked disorders (new)", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Disorder</strong>", "<strong>Cause</strong>", "<strong>Key points</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>Fragile X</strong>", "FMR1 CGG expansion (&gt;200 + methylation = full mutation; 55–200 = premutation)", "Long face, large ears, macro-orchidism, ID, autism features; anticipation via maternal transmission; premutation: FXTAS, ovarian insufficiency; test CGG repeats"], ["<strong>Duchenne</strong>", "X-linked dystrophin mutation (two thirds deletion/duplication)", "Gowers sign, calf pseudohypertrophy, CK &gt;10,000; MLPA/array first, then sequencing; about a third de novo"], ["<strong>Myotonic dystrophy 1</strong>", "DMPK CTG expansion", "Congenital form via maternal transmission; anticipation"], ["<strong>Huntington</strong>", "HTT CAG expansion", "Paternal anticipation; juvenile form with rigidity"]]}]}, {"title": "A12. Other trisomies and Robertsonian recurrence (new)", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trisomy 18 (Edwards):</strong> clenched overlapping fingers, rocker-bottom feet, small jaw, VSD, severe growth restriction. <strong>Trisomy 13 (Patau):</strong> holoprosencephaly, cleft lip/palate, postaxial polydactyly, scalp cutis aplasia, microphthalmia. Both carry high early mortality. <strong>Robertsonian recurrence (textbook empirical ⚑ verify):</strong> maternal 14;21 carrier about 10–15% for translocation Down; paternal carrier about 1–2%; a 21;21 carrier near 100%. Free trisomy 21 recurrence is about 1% plus the age-related risk. NIPT (cfDNA) is a screening test with false positives, so a high-risk result needs diagnostic confirmation (CVS or amniocentesis)."}]}, {"title": "A13. Hyperammonaemia drugs (new)", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Sodium benzoate conjugates with glycine (hippurate) and sodium phenylacetate with glutamine (phenylacetylglutamine); both are renally excreted and provide an alternative nitrogen route. Arginine is given in most UCDs but not in arginase deficiency. Carglumic acid activates CPS1 in NAGS deficiency. A usual IV loading dose is about 250 mg/kg of each scavenger over 90–120 minutes in dextrose ⚑ verify the local protocol. Scavengers bridge; dialysis planning continues at severe levels."}]}, {"title": "A14. Homocystinuria and CAH (new)", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Classic homocystinuria (CBS deficiency):</strong> marfanoid habitus, downward ectopia lentis, thromboembolism, intellectual disability; homocysteine and methionine raised; some respond to pyridoxine. Marfan lens dislocation is upward and homocysteine is normal. <strong>CAH (21-hydroxylase):</strong> salt-wasting crisis at 1–3 weeks with hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, hypoglycaemia; XX infants are virilised; screen with 17-hydroxyprogesterone (programme-specific); treat with saline, dextrose and hydrocortisone after drawing 17-OHP."}]}, {"title": "A15. Mitochondrial syndromes (new)", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "<strong>MELAS:</strong> stroke-like episodes not matching a vascular territory, lactic acidosis, deafness, diabetes; usually m.3243A&gt;G; maternal family pattern with variable heteroplasmy. <strong>Leigh syndrome:</strong> infant or early-childhood regression with basal ganglia and brainstem lesions; many nuclear (e.g. SURF1) causes, so inheritance may be autosomal recessive. Lactate may be normal between episodes."}]}, {"title": "A16. Counselling and Indian context (new)", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Genetic counselling covers recurrence risk, testing limits, incidental findings, prenatal options and consent; family testing is needed for balanced translocations, X-linked and repeat-expansion disorders. India has no single universal expanded newborn panel; programmes vary by state and institution, and congenital hypothyroidism screening follows ISPAE ⚑ verify current national programmes. 17-OHP, G6PD and biotinidase are commonly added to panels but depend on the programme."}]}]}, {"id": "cases", "title": "Clinical Cases", "group": "Practice", "kind": "cases", "sections": [{"title": "Case 1 — Global developmental delay with dysmorphism", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "A 19-month-old has microcephaly, delayed walking, a congenital renal anomaly and unusual ears. Pregnancy was uncomplicated. Parents are non-consanguineous; examination finds two minor anomalies and a structural renal defect. There is no single strongly recognizable syndrome."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Reasoning:</strong> Characterize phenotype and pedigree; select a genomic test capable of CNVs (CMA) and consider trio exome/genome early according to setting and local availability. Organ-specific screening and developmental care should proceed while testing is pending."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trap:</strong> A normal karyotype cannot exclude a pathogenic microdeletion."}]}, {"title": "Case 2 — Parent-of-origin interpretation", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "A 4-year-old was hypotonic with feeding difficulty in infancy, later developed hyperphagia and rapid weight gain. 15q methylation shows only the maternal imprint; SNP array shows maternal isodisomy without deletion."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Reasoning:</strong> Prader-Willi due to maternal UPD15; counsel that methylation establishes abnormal imprinting and SNP data clarify mechanism. Distinguish from Angelman due to maternal neuronal UBE3A deficiency."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trap:</strong> A 15q abnormality alone does not specify which imprinting syndrome without parental origin."}]}, {"title": "Case 3 — Conotruncal defect and low calcium", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "A 2-day-old with truncus arteriosus has jitteriness and low ionized calcium. Chest radiograph shows a small thymic shadow; there is a bifid uvula."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Reasoning:</strong> Consider 22q11.2 deletion; check calcium, PTH, T-cell subsets/function, palate and hearing; confirm by CMA. Assess live-vaccine suitability by immune phenotype, not the deletion label alone."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trap:</strong> Normal conventional karyotype does not exclude 22q11.2 microdeletion."}]}, {"title": "Case 4 — Neonatal respiratory alkalosis", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "A 3-day-old previously well infant develops poor feeding and lethargy. HR 172, RR 72; pH 7.51, PCO2 25, HCO3 20; glucose 88, lactate 1.7, urine ketones absent; ammonia 520 micromol/L."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Reasoning:</strong> Treat as severe hyperammonemia, strongly suspect UCD; stabilize airway, start anti-catabolic calories, call metabolic/ICU teams and arrange nitrogen scavengers and extracorporeal clearance planning while collecting amino acids and orotic acid."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trap:</strong> Normal glucose and near-normal lactate do not make the infant safe."}]}, {"title": "Case 5 — Ketotic organic-acid crisis", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "A 6-day-old has vomiting, hypotension and encephalopathy. pH 7.13, HCO3 9, anion gap 27, large ketones, glucose 55, ammonia 190 and neutropenia; newborn screen C3 is elevated."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Reasoning:</strong> PA/MMA are high in differential. Manage shock, glucose and infection risk while obtaining acylcarnitines, urine organic acids, MMA, homocysteine and B12 status; treat hyperammonemia if clinically indicated."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trap:</strong> An elevated ammonia can be secondary to organic acidemia; it does not automatically indicate OTC deficiency."}]}, {"title": "Case 6 — Fast-induced hypoglycemia", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "A 16-month-old with a viral illness has not eaten for 12 hours. He is drowsy with glucose 34 mg/dL, minimal ketones, mildly elevated transaminases and C8 elevation on prior screen."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Reasoning:</strong> Suspect MCAD deficiency; urgently provide glucose and avoid fasting, obtain metabolic specialist advice and confirmatory acylcarnitine/molecular testing."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trap:</strong> Do not wait for a second low glucose or assume low ketones excludes illness."}]}, {"title": "Case 7 — Metabolic screening result", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "A 48-hour dried-blood-spot sample is positive for C3 elevation in a well infant. The parents are anxious and ask if the baby definitely has propionic acidemia."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Reasoning:</strong> Explain that C3 is a screening signal. Arrange timely metabolic assessment and biochemical differentiation of PA, MMA, cobalamin-related disorders and maternal B12 deficiency; provide illness precautions."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trap:</strong> A screening marker cannot alone distinguish propionic from methylmalonic acidemia."}]}, {"title": "Case 8 — A normal screen with a sick baby", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "A 5-day-old has seizures, ammonia 580 micromol/L, respiratory alkalosis and little ketosis. A metabolic NBS panel had been reported normal on day 3."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Reasoning:</strong> The current phenotype overrides screening reassurance. Treat hyperammonemia immediately and evaluate UCD while addressing sepsis/other critical differentials."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trap:</strong> Screening coverage, sampling time and analytical sensitivity are limited; negative NBS is not an emergency rule-out."}]}]}, {"id": "data", "title": "Data Interpretation", "group": "Practice", "kind": "data", "sections": [{"title": "", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Charts and tables are simulated teaching data. Answers are in Section C."}]}, {"title": "Station 1 — Blood gas and ammonia panel", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Question:</strong> Classify each neonate’s metabolic pattern, compute the anion gap for N2 and name the first investigations."}, {"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Neonate</strong>", "<strong>pH</strong>", "<strong>PCO₂</strong>", "<strong>HCO₃</strong>", "<strong>Na / Cl</strong>", "<strong>Glucose</strong>", "<strong>Ketones</strong>", "<strong>Ammonia</strong>", "<strong>Other</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>N1</strong>", "7.53", "22", "18", "138 / 104", "70", "Negative", "480", "Lethargic, tachypnoeic"], ["<strong>N2</strong>", "7.12", "24", "9", "138 / 102", "55", "Large", "190", "Neutropenia, C3 raised"], ["<strong>N3</strong>", "7.38", "38", "22", "139 / 103", "34", "Very low BOHB", "60", "Hepatomegaly, AST mildly raised"]]}]}, {"title": "Station 2 — Acylcarnitine profiles", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Question:</strong> Name the most likely disorder for each profile and the clinical priority."}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/37e2746d9c85b5a87691eeb325b5e520bb28f5cc.png", "alt": "Station 2"}]}, {"title": "Station 3 — Copy-number plot", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Question:</strong> A child has a conotruncal defect and hypocalcaemia. Interpret the plot, name the syndrome and list the next steps."}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/1bb3cca6002f8340d4246afefc8cb7dd421cd16e_q.png", "alt": ""}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/1bb3cca6002f8340d4246afefc8cb7dd421cd16e.png", "alt": "Station 3", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}]}, {"title": "Station 4 — Imprinting and SNP-array data", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Question:</strong> Interpret each patient and name the mechanism."}, {"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Patient</strong>", "<strong>Phenotype</strong>", "<strong>SNRPN methylation</strong>", "<strong>SNP array</strong>", "<strong>Other</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>P1</strong>", "Hypotonia, feeding difficulty, later hyperphagia", "Maternal band only", "Deletion 15q11.2-q13", "—"], ["<strong>P2</strong>", "Same phenotype", "Maternal band only", "No deletion; maternal isodisomy", "—"], ["<strong>P3</strong>", "Severe speech impairment, ataxia, seizures, happy demeanour", "Normal", "Normal", "UBE3A sequence variant on exome"]]}]}, {"title": "Station 5 — Urea-cycle biomarker signatures", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Question:</strong> Match each block to its citrulline and orotic acid signature; which defect has low citrulline with high orotic acid?"}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/26750f751abe9345c88003260a8f15cd18334e5a_q.png", "alt": ""}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/26750f751abe9345c88003260a8f15cd18334e5a.png", "alt": "Station 5", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}]}, {"title": "Station 6 — Newborn-screen decisions", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Question:</strong> State the action for each infant."}, {"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Infant</strong>", "<strong>Screen result</strong>", "<strong>Clinical state</strong>", "<strong>Other data</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>I1</strong>", "C3 raised", "Well, feeding normally", "Mother vegan"], ["<strong>I2</strong>", "TSH &gt;40 mIU/L (serum-equivalent) at 60 h", "Well", "Venous sample not yet taken"], ["<strong>I3</strong>", "Normal panel on day 3", "Day 5: seizures, respiratory alkalosis", "Ammonia 580 µmol/L"]]}]}]}, {"id": "revision", "title": "Rapid Revision", "group": "Practice", "kind": "revision", "sections": [{"title": "", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["Choose the test by variant class: karyotype for balanced rearrangements, CMA for CNVs, methylation for imprinting, exome/genome for monogenic variants, repeat analysis for expansions.", "PWS = paternal loss; Angelman = maternal UBE3A loss; 22q11.2 = conotruncal, calcium, palate, variable immune phenotype.", "Down AVSD/TAM; Turner coarctation; Williams SVAS; Noonan pulmonary stenosis; Fragile X CGG; DMD dystrophin.", "Sick neonate: gas, ammonia, glucose, ketones and lactate before diagnostic anchoring; treat catabolism first.", "Respiratory alkalosis + high ammonia = UCD; ketotic acidosis = organic acidaemia; hypoketotic fasting hypoglycaemia = FAOD.", "OTC low citrulline/high orotic; CPS1/NAGS low citrulline/low orotic; ASS1 high citrulline; ASL argininosuccinate.", "MSUD alloisoleucine; PA/MMA C3; MCAD C8; VLCAD C14:1; tyrosinaemia I succinylacetone.", "Newborn screening is a prompt for action, never proof or a rule-out in a symptomatic baby."]}]}]}, {"id": "quizzes", "title": "Quizzes", "group": "Practice", "kind": "quizzes", "sections": []}, {"id": "recall", "title": "Active Recall", "group": "Revise", "kind": "recall", "sections": [{"title": "", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": true, "items": ["Which test detects a CGG repeat expansion?<br>Answer: FMR1 repeat analysis (karyotype and microarray do not).", "First genetic test in a boy with Gowers sign and CK 15,000?<br>Answer: DMD deletion/duplication analysis (MLPA/array), then sequencing.", "Trisomy 13 vs 18 clues?<br>Answer: T13: holoprosencephaly, cleft, polydactyly; T18: clenched overlapping fingers, rocker-bottom feet.", "Mechanism of sodium benzoate?<br>Answer: Conjugates glycine to form hippurate, an alternative nitrogen excretion route.", "Homocystinuria biochemistry?<br>Answer: Raised homocysteine and methionine (CBS deficiency); lens dislocates downward.", "First treatment of salt-wasting CAH?<br>Answer: IV saline, dextrose and hydrocortisone, after drawing 17-OHP.", "MELAS inheritance clue?<br>Answer: Maternal transmission of mtDNA m.3243A&gt;G with heteroplasmy.", "Three ETROP Type 1 categories.<br>Answer: ETROP Type 1: zone I any stage with plus; zone I stage 3 with or without plus; zone II stage 2 or 3 with plus.", "Stage 1 versus 2 versus 3 morphology.<br>Answer: Stage 1 = demarcation line; stage 2 = elevated ridge; stage 3 = extraretinal fibrovascular proliferation.", "ICROP3 plus versus preplus.<br>Answer: ICROP3 describes a continuum: plus has sufficiently severe posterior vascular dilation and tortuosity; preplus is abnormal but insufficient for plus.", "Type 2 ROP follow-up patterns.<br>Answer: Type 2 examples: zone I stage 1 or 2 without plus, or zone II stage 3 without plus; close surveillance rather than automatic Type 1 treatment.", "AAP 2018 first exam at GA 24 weeks.<br>Answer: AAP 2018 schedule: infant born at 24 weeks GA has first exam at 31 weeks PMA, i.e., 7 weeks chronological age.", "Anti-VEGF late recurrence surveillance.<br>Answer: After anti-VEGF, late reactivation and persistent avascular retina may occur; prolonged specialist retinal follow-up is essential.", "PVHI versus historical grade IV IVH.<br>Answer: PVHI is a periventricular venous hemorrhagic infarction associated with severe IVH; it is not simply parenchymal extension of ventricular blood.", "CPS HUS schedule for ≤31+6 weeks.<br>Answer: CPS statement (2020, reaffirmed 12 Jan 2026): GA &lt;=31+6 weeks routine HUS at 4–7 days and repeat at 4–6 weeks; abnormal grade &gt;=2 IVH or WMI needs earlier repeat 7–10 days.", "Term-equivalent HUS indication under CPS.<br>Answer: CPS: term-equivalent HUS routinely for GA &lt;26 weeks; for GA 26+0–31+6 weeks when moderate/severe earlier lesions or additional risk factors exist.", "VI, AHW, TOD definitions.<br>Answer: VI = ventricular index (midline to lateral ventricular border at foramen of Monro); AHW = anterior horn width; TOD = thalamo-occipital distance, used to follow PHVD.", "Timing of PVL cyst appearance.<br>Answer: Cystic PVL commonly evolves over 2–6 weeks after insult; serial HUS is needed because early images can miss cysts.", "Best modality for noncystic WMI.<br>Answer: MRI is more sensitive than cranial ultrasound for diffuse noncystic white-matter injury.", "Motor phenotype after cystic PVL.<br>Answer: Cystic PVL is classically associated with spastic diplegic cerebral palsy, especially lower-limb involvement."]}, {"t": "callout", "label": "FACTS TO RECALL", "title": "", "html": ["The document gives no question for these lines; read and recall them."], "fill": ""}, {"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["Karyotype for balanced structural changes; CMA for CNVs; exome/genome for many sequence variants.", "Parental segregation helps de novo, recessive and compound heterozygous interpretation.", "PWS loses paternal 15q gene expression; Angelman loses maternal neuronal UBE3A.", "Conotruncal cardiac, hypocalcemia, variable T-cell deficiency; CMA detects deletion.", "Williams syndrome (7q11.23 ELN-region deletion).", "Major affects health/function; minor has limited standalone impact; sequence has a causal cascade; association is nonrandom without known unified cause.", "UCD ammonia + respiratory alkalosis; OA ketotic AG acidosis; FAOD hypoketotic fasting hypoglycemia.", "Resuscitate, draw critical samples if feasible, IV calories, metabolic ICU contact, treat sepsis as indicated.", "Both low citrulline; OTC high urinary orotic acid, CPS1/NAGS low or normal.", "Marked citrulline.", "Argininosuccinic acid.", "Arginase 1 deficiency.", "Carglumic acid, a CPS1 activator analog.", "Alloisoleucine and succinylacetone, respectively.", "PA, MMA, cobalamin-processing/B12-related conditions; confirm with urine organic acids and MMA/homocysteine.", "MCAD and VLCAD, respectively.", "Pompe infantile HCM/hypotonia; GSD I fasting hypoglycemia, lactate, urate and triglycerides.", "Vertical supranuclear gaze palsy with neurovisceral disease.", "Panel limits, timing and false negatives; symptomatic biochemical emergency overrides screening.", "&gt;40 mIU/L serum-equivalent screening TSH requires prompt venous FT4/T4 and TSH under ISPAE 2018."], "facts": true}]}]}, {"id": "notebook", "title": "Error Notebook", "group": "Revise", "kind": "notebook", "sections": []}, {"id": "plan", "title": "Revision Plan", "group": "Revise", "kind": "plan", "sections": [{"title": "Self-Assessment & Spaced Review", "h1": "Self-Assessment & Spaced Review", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Component</strong>", "<strong>Score</strong>", "<strong>Action</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>Base MCQs</strong>", "____ / 75", "Green ≥85%; amber 70–84%; red &lt;70% (indicative)."], ["<strong>Carry-over retests</strong>", "____ / 2", "Log separately."], ["<strong>Gap-fill extras</strong>", "____ / 8", "Review A11–A16 for each miss."], ["<strong>Data stations</strong>", "____ / 6", "Redo the calculations and plot reads."], ["<strong>Active recall</strong>", "____ / 27", "Repeat misses tomorrow."], ["<strong>Calculation drill</strong>", "____ / 7", "Redo errors with formula and units."]]}, {"t": "p", "html": "RAG: GREEN = correct with clear reasoning; AMBER = correct by guess or between close distractors; RED = wrong or unexplained. Spaced review: R1 about 24 h, R2 about 7 days, R3 about 21 days."}]}]}, {"id": "references", "title": "References", "group": "More", "kind": "notes", "sections": [{"title": "Sources & Verification Status", "h1": "Sources & Verification Status", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Claim</strong>", "<strong>Status</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>Test selection by variant class; ACMG 2021 exome/genome as first- or second-tier</strong>", "International guideline as quoted; consistent with standard practice."], ["<strong>CHOP hyperammonaemia categories and glucose infusion</strong>", "US institutional pathway as quoted; not universal thresholds ⚑"], ["<strong>ISPAE 2018 CH screening timing, TSH &gt;40, LT4 10–15 µg/kg/day</strong>", "Published Indian guideline as quoted; assay-specific ⚑"], ["<strong>Biomarker patterns (citrulline, orotic acid, C3/C8/C14:1, alloisoleucine, succinylacetone)</strong>", "Standard textbook / GeneReviews."], ["<strong>Robertsonian recurrence 10–15% / 1–2%</strong>", "⚑ Textbook empirical estimates; vary by source."], ["<strong>Scavenger loading dose 250 mg/kg; ammonia conversion factor</strong>", "⚑ Local protocol and laboratory dependent."], ["<strong>Indian newborn-screening programme scope</strong>", "⚑ Varies by state/institution; not verified."], ["<strong>Carry-over retests and recall (Days 7, 8, 11)</strong>", "Verify against the original day sources; CPS and AAP items are international."]]}, {"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22nd ed.; Cloherty &amp; Stark’s Manual of Neonatal Care.", "ACMG 2021 exome/genome guideline: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41436-021-01242-6 ; ACMG ACT sheets: https://www.acmg.net/act-sheets-and-algorithms/", "CHOP Neonatal Hyperammonemia Clinical Pathway: https://www.chop.edu/clinical-pathway/neonatal-hyperammonemia-clinical-pathway", "ISPAE 2018 congenital hypothyroidism guideline: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29380252/", "GeneReviews: Prader-Willi, Angelman, 22q11.2 deletion, Williams, MCAD and MSUD (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK1330/ and companion chapters).", "Canadian Paediatric Society routine imaging of the preterm brain (carry-over): https://cps.ca/en/documents/position/routine-imaging-of-preterm-neonatal-brain", "Doses and thresholds are exam anchors; local protocols and specialist judgement govern care."]}, {"t": "p", "html": "CONSOLIDATION AND QUALITY-CONTROL NOTES"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Comparison basis: original generated Day 15 edition (77 questions with expanded four-option rationales) and supplied Day 15 V4 (85 questions, expanded topic coverage, 3 diagrams, 7 calculations, cases and data stations). V4 is the core for this consolidated learner edition."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Confirmed answer-key corrections: Q8 = A (cfDNA is a screening result requiring diagnostic confirmation); Q12 = A (systematic dysmorphology assessment, family history and anomaly survey). Both the question-level key and quick key were corrected."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Option-specific rationale was incorporated from the previous edition only when option matching was sufficiently reliable. Unmatched revised choices are supported by the V4 answer/key/core reasoning and trap text, not represented as independently validated four-option rationales."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Question arithmetic: 75 base + 2 carry-over neonatal retests + 8 gap-fill questions = 85 total. Retests and extras are separate from the 75 base score. Neonatal carry-over recalls remain separate."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source caution: Some V4 notes explicitly flag unverified thresholds, doses and attributions. In particular, hyperammonaemia scavenger dosing, dialysis escalation thresholds, ammonia-unit conversion, screening TSH and Robertsonian recurrence risks must be checked against a dated and applicable protocol before clinical use. CHOP is a US institutional protocol; ACMG is international; Indian IAP/IAP-NNF and national guidance should be prioritized when relevant."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Textbook access: This comparison used the two supplied DOCX study editions; it does not imply direct verification against full Nelson 22e, OP Ghai, Cloherty/Stark, paid Marrow or PrepLadder material."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Review status: Content/structure reconciliation and key cross-checking completed, but four full independent specialist-level medical review passes and source-by-source verification of all 85 questions have NOT been completed. Accordingly, this file is a consolidated study edition with disclosed open verification items, rather than a fully validated clinical reference."}]}]}], "quizSets": [{"k": 1, "from": 1, "to": 17, "n": 17}, {"k": 2, "from": 18, "to": 34, "n": 17}, {"k": 3, "from": 35, "to": 51, "n": 17}, {"k": 4, "from": 52, "to": 68, "n": 17}, {"k": 5, "from": 69, "to": 85, "n": 17}], "mcqCount": 85, "calc": {"n": 7, "href": "calc.html"}};
